  "worked_solution": "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{y}^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{4} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{4} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059137/assets/c1e092dd6302bb5948c6452e43b093fc3a531132580c1bb1e42fbe846152f63c.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=16{y}^{4}-1$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}(16{y}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(16{y}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =32\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{0}^{1/2}-2\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}(\\frac{1}{32}-0)-2(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-\\frac{4}{5}|=\\frac{4}{5}.$",
